WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.260
Welcome to this explainer. You know, as a product manager, you face one absolute ultimate challenge every single day. And it's this. Figuring out if a problem is actually real before you go and spend your team's valuable time and money building the solution. So today, we're going to unlock exactly how to do that by escaping what I like to call the polite feedback trap. OK, let's dive into this. Picture this scenario for a second. You pitch your exciting new feature to a user and they smile widely and tell you, wow, I'd

2
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.640
totally use that product. It sounds brilliant. Now, hearing this kind of enthusiastic feedback

3
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.380
feels amazing, right? It feels like instant validation. You hit the nail on the head.

4
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.040
But here's the hard truth. It is actually a massive trap for PMs. Because let's be blunt,

5
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.700
this is worthless data. Seriously. Most people, much like a supportive mother,

6
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.280
will just sugarcoat their answers out of sheer politeness. They don't want to hurt your feelings

7
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.440
when you're clearly so excited about your new idea.

8
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.420
Because of this deeply ingrained human urge

9
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.540
to be polite and supportive,

10
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.540
their future promises to use or buy your product

11
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.660
are completely useless for true validation.

12
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.360
So let's look at section one, the VLOX trap,

13
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.720
or when 78% means absolutely nothing.

14
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.380
Now, what's really interesting about this slide

15
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.200
is how easy it is to get fooled.

16
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.040
Let's look at a real-world example

17
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.500
with a product manager named McCall over at Velux.

18
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.140
She almost fell right into this polite feedback trap

19
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.700
while researching demand for a new shared schedule feature.

20
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.800
She sent out a simple survey asking,

21
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.360
would you want a shared schedule?

22
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.220
And bam, 78% said yes.

23
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.160
Looking at this overwhelming optimism,

24
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.160
you'd probably start building immediately, right?

25
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.380
But optimism about the future isn't a fact.

26
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.180
It's just a pleasant guess.

27
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.520
Fortunately, Michael realized this.

28
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.380
She changed her approach entirely

29
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.260
and pivoted to one-on-one interviews.

30
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.340
And by doing that,

31
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.820
she uncovered the true, incredibly underwhelming facts.

32
00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.260
By asking how these users currently manage their schedules,

33
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.620
she found out that only three out of eight team leads even had the problem to begin with.

34
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.700
And of those three, they only spent about 20 minutes a week dealing with it.

35
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.800
It barely even registered as a pain point.

36
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.560
Discovering this reality saved her team three whole months of wasted development.

37
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.940
Principles of honest data.

38
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.280
Getting the truth without asking for it.

39
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.540
The core philosophy here is recognizing that you literally cannot force people to be honest with you.

40
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.800
But you absolutely can change how you ask your questions.

41
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.420
Here are your three golden rules to make that happen.

42
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.360
First, talk about their life, not your idea.

43
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:38.360
The second you put your own idea on the table, people immediately switch into that polite, optimistic mode we talked about.

44
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.900
Second, ask about specifics in the past, not opinions about the future.

45
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.840
The past is factual, the future is just a fantasy.

46
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.600
And third, listen more and talk less.

47
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.940
Every single minute you spend pitching is a minute you aren't learning.

48
00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.330
And this brilliantly illustrates the difference in action.

49
00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.210
On the left, we have a bad hypothetical question.

50
00:02:58.630 --> 00:02:59.990
Would you use a shared schedule?

51
00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.530
It practically begs for those pleasant lies.

52
00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.750
On the right, we have a good fact-based question.

53
00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.730
How do you coordinate your team today?

54
00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:08.870
You see the difference?

55
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.890
This anchors the user in reality.

56
00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:16.570
It forces them to give you concrete data about their actual behavior rather than just feeding you empty compliments.

57
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.460
Spotting the currencies.

58
00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.000
This is all about moving from compliments to actual commitments.

59
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.280
So the crucial point is this.

60
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.540
If you walk out of a meeting and the user just says, sounds great, let me know when it's ready.

61
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.620
That is a polite escape route.

62
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.380
That's not a tangible next step.

63
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.860
You need to test for true commitment using one of three currencies.

64
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.140
Time means they will book a follow-up meeting with you right now.

65
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.080
Reputation means they'll put their personal name on the line, like maybe giving you their manager's email.

66
00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.320
And money, well, that is the absolute strongest evidence.

67
00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.100
like actually paying to join a beta.

68
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.780
McCall used this exact concept later on.

69
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.700
When she pivoted her whole idea to a workload dashboard,

70
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.480
she didn't just ask if they liked it.

71
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.400
Nope, she asked for their time

72
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.440
and successfully got five team leads

73
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.940
to actively pilot the new feature.

74
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.700
That is real validation.

75
00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:05.370
Ready to dive in?

76
00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.970
Let's move to and see how this builds

77
00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.570
into your next steps in discovery.

78
00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.050
Uncovering the uncomfortable truth

79
00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.930
rather than just happily chasing compliments

80
00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:15.430
is your real job here.

81
00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:22.590
It is all about having the intellectual honesty to prefer a hard fact over a warm, fuzzy opinion.

82
00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.790
So, I'm going to leave you with this provocative challenge.

83
00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.190
Are your users giving you facts, or are they just being polite?

84
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:31.490
It's time to find out.

85
00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:37.430
I invite you to jump right into the reading for this course stage so you can master these exact conversation techniques.

86
00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:43.610
Stop guessing, avoid the polite trap for good, and start building products that solve real, actual problems.

87
00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.410
Get in there, and let's get learning.

88
00:04:45.590 --> 00:05:15.570
Thank you.

